[{:db/id "q-333365/selection/f90af16d53b64f18d625ff3eace399df056a4e71194606e0c73388c7e67a5f9c" :answer/id #uuid "16a59d8a-69e3-52c5-aa62-d8170533b658" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Stable but not asymptotically stable." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333365/selection/fcda0b7bf263dd68eee7b8c102aa23e51e5c82e7cc8286ce388d069475b06d03" :answer/id #uuid "befa8d07-e937-5866-bf06-12a6368ff3f4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Asymptotically stable only for nonlinear systems." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333365/selection/e8d2fcfd3dcf338a56b83168409d2c7e88677b6bccdcc7cb33a059689ac93d44" :answer/id #uuid "767723fb-d32d-5a40-8828-30177dc2a0cd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Asymptotically unstable." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333365/selection/efa085861614fb969d9d1eeb912156eeea84a9f563c14f4b3ebaea31dc8dd746" :answer/id #uuid "e019e31f-ebc1-5bab-a719-499a4ea439c1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Asymptotically stable." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333365/selection/8de973a8b722c5184b720214154411f6f9a4bc3509a1d33cca03e3004992ee9d" :answer/id #uuid "dfc6b856-4f58-5f40-bbc1-d8c18ae5096a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Unstable." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333365/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f82aee8-df8d-5697-a473-6e7ab9e13456" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-333365/selection/efa085861614fb969d9d1eeb912156eeea84a9f563c14f4b3ebaea31dc8dd746" :answer-field/choices ["q-333365/selection/f90af16d53b64f18d625ff3eace399df056a4e71194606e0c73388c7e67a5f9c" "q-333365/selection/fcda0b7bf263dd68eee7b8c102aa23e51e5c82e7cc8286ce388d069475b06d03" "q-333365/selection/e8d2fcfd3dcf338a56b83168409d2c7e88677b6bccdcc7cb33a059689ac93d44" "q-333365/selection/efa085861614fb969d9d1eeb912156eeea84a9f563c14f4b3ebaea31dc8dd746" "q-333365/selection/8de973a8b722c5184b720214154411f6f9a4bc3509a1d33cca03e3004992ee9d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333365" :question/id #uuid "638a5026-be36-5dd1-908b-d2c6d418484f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333365" :question/problem "A small perturbation away from an equilibrium causes trajectories to slowly spiral towards the equilibrium. How should the equilibrium be classified?" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"Asymptotically stable.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333365/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333365"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-335454/selection/0257391ab78f2c81a7fca3ffbab56aa9d830298b09f6bc780184b0dc1472baf5" :answer/id #uuid "8e970316-5a61-5305-b09d-9d723ec78dd4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Solutions approach the equilibrium only if the system is linear and all eigenvalues are real." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335454/selection/f2a879580d256a20ee2272e03efb237c04eed9d9b5456fea2972f4d1bee7c2c3" :answer/id #uuid "c3c8ae98-0576-5bd5-b062-7e93d61fdb59" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Solutions that start anywhere in the state space eventually remain in some fixed neighborhood of the equilibrium." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335454/selection/0c0102e6a3d8542524eb7baaa545d4095a27f576d713767139f26bedd1f0d07d" :answer/id #uuid "f07062c7-4321-5bd2-b573-99e7bf77edab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Some solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually leave a fixed neighborhood of it." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335454/selection/ea515f63b3727edfce01f85a7643c7ef99c133109cdb8d4b37657c21de738378" :answer/id #uuid "c0b0c803-30b8-5d6e-bb2a-ba067c853c5b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Solutions that start close to the equilibrium remain close to it for all future times, but may not converge to it." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335454/selection/6c47b6c954c7f152c4e492f4f76225dfc2cdf3b5b6ed263d5fd6adb2c3d21252" :answer/id #uuid "00583e6a-7009-5d81-8088-2b171ea09df8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335454/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ae10e357-6c37-5be1-a5f0-4f24aee3d8d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-335454/selection/0c0102e6a3d8542524eb7baaa545d4095a27f576d713767139f26bedd1f0d07d" :answer-field/choices ["q-335454/selection/0257391ab78f2c81a7fca3ffbab56aa9d830298b09f6bc780184b0dc1472baf5" "q-335454/selection/f2a879580d256a20ee2272e03efb237c04eed9d9b5456fea2972f4d1bee7c2c3" "q-335454/selection/0c0102e6a3d8542524eb7baaa545d4095a27f576d713767139f26bedd1f0d07d" "q-335454/selection/ea515f63b3727edfce01f85a7643c7ef99c133109cdb8d4b37657c21de738378" "q-335454/selection/6c47b6c954c7f152c4e492f4f76225dfc2cdf3b5b6ed263d5fd6adb2c3d21252"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335454" :question/id #uuid "894667d1-f4aa-5975-a79e-d7ee80410cd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-335454" :question/problem "Which statement correctly describes an **unstable** equilibrium point?" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nSome solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually leave a fixed neighborhood of it." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335454/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335454"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-335463/field-1/d75f4c3d0456929d4b5eb03d1